---
import Learn from '../../layouts/Learn.astro';
import MemoryFlow from '../../components/learn/MemoryFlow.astro';
import BarChart from '../../components/learn/BarChart.astro';

// Learn article 5 (s91). Facts read on 2026-10-06:
// - OpenAI, "Exporting your ChatGPT history and data" (help.openai.com/en/
//   articles/7260999), updated about September 2026: Settings, Data controls,
//   Export data, Export, Confirm export; Free, Go, Plus, Pro; up to 7 days to
//   arrive; the download link expires 24 hours after it arrives; a ZIP with
//   chat history.
// - OpenAI, "Memory in ChatGPT" (help.openai.com/en/articles/8590148).
// - Anthropic, "Import and export your memory from Claude"
//   (support.claude.com/en/articles/12123587), updated 2026-09-02: Free, Pro,
//   Max, Team; Settings, Memory, Start import; experimental; up to 24 hours;
//   work-related topics kept mainly.
// First-hand: session 64 (2026-09-26): a meeting preference saved in ChatGPT
// (Work) was recalled by Claude once told to check Zaaheen; session 82
// (2026-10-02, Zaaheen 0.3.0): a memory saved in Claude was recalled by
// ChatGPT Desktop among other apps.
const OUTLINE = [
  { id: 'three', title: 'Three things to bring' },
  { id: 'memory', title: 'What ChatGPT remembers' },
  { id: 'chats', title: 'Your old chats' },
  { id: 'time', title: 'How long it takes' },
  { id: 'again', title: 'Never move it again' },
];
const OPENAI_EXPORT = 'https://help.openai.com/en/articles/7260999-exporting-your-chatgpt-history-and-data';
const OPENAI_MEMORY = 'https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8590148-memory-in-chatgpt';
const CLAUDE_IMPORT = 'https://support.claude.com/en/articles/12123587-import-and-export-your-memory-from-claude';
---
<Learn
  path="/learn/switch-from-chatgpt-to-claude/"
  lede="Bring three things: what ChatGPT remembers about you, which Claude's memory import takes in a few minutes; your custom instructions, which you copy by hand; and your old chats, which ChatGPT can export for you as a download. To avoid doing it all again next time, keep what's true about you in a memory outside both apps."
  outline={OUTLINE}
>
  <section id="three">
    <h2>Three things to bring</h2>
    <table class="learn-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">What</th><th scope="col">How it moves</th><th scope="col">Lands in Claude as</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">What ChatGPT remembers about you</th><td>Claude's memory import</td><td>Claude's memory</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Your custom instructions</th><td>Copy and paste</td><td>Your preferences in Claude's settings</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Your old chats</th><td>ChatGPT's data export</td><td>A file you keep, to look things up</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="memory">
    <h2>What ChatGPT remembers about you</h2>
    <p>This is the part people mean by "starting over", and Claude has a tool for it
       (<a href={CLAUDE_IMPORT}>Anthropic's help page</a>), on the Free, Pro, Max and Team plans:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>In Claude, open Settings, then Memory, and choose Start import. Copy the prompt Claude
          gives you.</li>
      <li>Paste it into ChatGPT. ChatGPT writes out what it remembers about you.</li>
      <li>Read it first, and take out anything you'd rather not carry over.</li>
      <li>Paste the rest into Claude's box and choose Add to memory.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Anthropic calls the import experimental, says it can take up to 24 hours to show up, and
       notes that Claude keeps mainly work-related details. Check Claude's memory the next day and
       add what's missing by telling Claude directly.</p>
    <p>Your custom instructions, the "how to answer me" text in ChatGPT's personalisation settings,
       don't come across with the memory. Copy them over by hand into Claude's own settings.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="chats">
    <h2>Your old chats</h2>
    <p>ChatGPT can send you a copy of everything (<a href={OPENAI_EXPORT}>OpenAI's help page</a>),
       on the Free, Go, Plus and Pro plans:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>In ChatGPT, open Settings, then Data controls.</li>
      <li>Under Export data, choose Export, then Confirm export.</li>
      <li>Wait for the email or text message, and download the file while you're signed in.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>You get a ZIP file with your chat history. Keep it as your archive: when you need an old
       answer, search the file, or paste the relevant part into a new Claude chat. Leaving
       ChatGPT? Its memory and chats stay in your OpenAI account until you delete them
       (<a href={OPENAI_MEMORY}>how ChatGPT's memory is stored and deleted</a>).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="time">
    <h2>How long it takes</h2>
    <p>Most of the work is a few minutes. The waiting is longer, so start the export first:</p>

    <BarChart
      id="chart-switch-wait"
      title="The longest each step can take, in hours, from each company's own help page"
      measure="Hours, at most"
      max={168}
      bars={[
        { label: 'ChatGPT data export', value: 168, note: 'hours (7 days)' },
        { label: 'Claude memory import', value: 24, note: 'hours' },
        { label: 'Export link stays open', value: 24, note: 'hours' },
      ]}
      source={{ text: "OpenAI, \"Exporting your ChatGPT history and data\", and Anthropic, \"Import and export your memory from Claude\", both read on 6 October 2026.", href: OPENAI_EXPORT }}
    />

    <p>The link in ChatGPT's email stops working 24 hours after it arrives, so download the file
       the day it comes.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="again">
    <h2>Never move it again</h2>
    <p>Every move between AI apps repeats the same job, because each one keeps its memory inside
       its own account. The way out is to keep what's true about you somewhere you own, and let
       each app read it.</p>

    <MemoryFlow
      id="flow-switch"
      from="ChatGPT"
      to="Claude"
      fact="Book meetings after 10am"
      caption="A preference saved while you used ChatGPT is kept on your computer, so Claude recalls it the day you switch, and any app you try after that."
    />

    <p>Zaaheen is that kind of memory, for Windows and Mac, encrypted on your own computer. Both
       directions work in our own tests: Claude recalled a meeting preference that ChatGPT had
       saved, once asked to check Zaaheen (26 September 2026), and ChatGPT recalled a memory
       Claude had saved (2 October 2026).
       Before you switch, connect both apps and ask ChatGPT once: "Save everything you remember
       about me to Zaaheen." The next time you change apps, there is nothing to move.</p>
  </section>
</Learn>
